                <pb facs="0792" n="782" sameAs="frus:frus1937v05/pg782"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.3131/118: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Venezuela</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Nicholson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-09-08T17:00:00-04:30">September 8, 1937—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:06 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>89. Department’s telegram No. 48, September 4, 3 p.m. The Foreign Minister in an
                    interview today expressed his perfect understanding of the Department’s point of
                    view and promised to study the matter in the hope of finding a satisfactory
                    solution. He intimated that he might be willing to propose as a compromise the
                    removal of the 30 percent surcharge from products originating in Puerto Rico and
                    the Virgin Islands while transshipped goods would continue to pay the surcharge.
                    I believe this is as far as he would be willing or able to go.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nicholson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
